<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1948 CE · capital Saigon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A French-sponsored separatist republic in southern Vietnam, set up in 1946 to keep the south apart from Ho Chi Minh's government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, High Commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-thierry-dargenlieu">Thierry d'Argenlieu</a> proclaimed the Autonomous Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-cochinchina">Cochinchina</a> on 1 June 1946, while negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam</a> were taking place in France. Its first president was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-van-thinh">Nguyen Van Thinh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, was dominated by French interests and a small southern Francophile elite. It had little popular support and was denounced by Vietnamese nationalists as a French puppet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-van-thinh">Nguyen Van Thinh</a>, unable to govern effectively, committed suicide in November 1946, and his successors were equally weak.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> changed strategy to work with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bao Dai</a>, the republic was renamed the Provisional Government of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-vietnam">South Vietnam</a> in 1947 and in 1948 merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-central-government-of-vietnam">Provisional Central Government of Vietnam</a>. In 1949 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-cochinchina">Cochinchina</a> was formally attached to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina.jpg" alt="Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saigon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nguyen Van Thinh, Le Van Hoach</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Catholicism, Cao Dai</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Cochinchina, Provisional Government of the Republic of Cochinchina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1947&amp;focus=autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina" aria-label="Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7619 1775 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7939 2031L7939 2031M7939 2031L5054 728M7939 2031L7939 2031"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7939 2031h0M5054 728h0M7939 2031h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7939 2031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7939 2031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saigon. <a href="/atlas?year=1947&amp;focus=autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
